  <!-- Article 5: Favicon Size & SEO Impact -->
  <article class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--border)] bg-[var(--card)] p-6 sm:p-8 shadow-lg space-y-4">
    <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[var(--foreground)] flex items-center gap-3">
      <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-rose-500/15 text-rose-400 font-mono text-sm flex items-center justify-center font-black">
        05
      </span>
      Why Favicon Size Matters for Search Engine Optimization (SEO)
    </h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--muted-foreground)] leading-relaxed">
      Many site owners overlook the connection between <strong>favicon size</strong> and search visibility. In modern search engine algorithms, your website icon plays an influential role in user experience and organic performance:
    </p>
    <div class="space-y-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-[var(--muted-foreground)] leading-relaxed">
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-cyan-500/30 bg-cyan-500/5">
        <strong class="text-[var(--foreground)] block mb-1">1. Improved Click-Through Rate (CTR):</strong>
        On mobile search results, Google places your favicon directly beside your site title and breadcrumb URL. A crisp, high-contrast, visually appealing favicon instantly draws user eyes, lifting your organic click-through rate over competitors with blurry or missing default icons.
      </div>
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-indigo-500/30 bg-indigo-500/5">
        <strong class="text-[var(--foreground)] block mb-1">2. Googlebot 48px Validation Rule:</strong>
        Google Search Central guidelines explicitly mandate that your favicon must be a multiple of 48 pixels square (such as 48×48, 96×96, or 144×144 px) to be indexed. Submitting non-square or undersized files causes Google to drop your icon and display an anonymous gray globe.
      </div>
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-emerald-500/30 bg-emerald-500/5">
        <strong class="text-[var(--foreground)] block mb-1">3. Enhanced Brand Credibility:</strong>
        A missing favicon is one of the quickest ways to trigger user hesitation. Visitors associate missing icons with phishing sites, incomplete staging builds, or abandoned domains. A polished icon fosters immediate brand recognition and dwell time.
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Article 6: How to Implement Your Favicon Suite -->
  <article class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--border)] bg-[var(--card)] p-6 sm:p-8 shadow-lg space-y-4">
    <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[var(--foreground)] flex items-center gap-3">
      <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-teal-500/15 text-teal-400 font-mono text-sm flex items-center justify-center font-black">
        06
